# Explanation: The Admission Boundary

Obsmetrologia starts with a deliberately thin floor. A mark is not yet an object, a
truth value, a subject, evidence, syntax, or order. Those structures are generated
later. Starting with less is what lets the later recovery claims mean something.

An utterance is the first structured carrier: token first, claim second. The
distinction is positional. It does not yet say that the token is a subject or the
claim is a predicate. Predication has not happened yet.

Articulating is the first constructive act. It admits the recursive shape that
lets utterances be brought into relation. This is the second and last primitive
admission in the foundation spine. Below articulating, the project should be able
to say what generated a structure instead of treating it as free.

The first recurring four appears because an utterance has two sides and each side
can be unarticulated or articulated. At this stage the four corners are
articulation statuses, not bilattice values. Positing later reads the same
four-point shape as value.

## Do Not Import Later Structure

The most common documentation error here is to use helpful words too early:
subject, object, truth, category, evidence, order. Each of those words is valid
only after the morphism that generates it. Primitive documentation should explain
why later structure is absent.

That absence is not poverty. It is what makes later recovery meaningful. If truth
or order were silently present at the floor, then positing and settling would be
renamings. Obsmetrologia needs the reader to feel the delay: first the mark, then the
utterance boundary, then the act of articulation, and only after that the lawful
arrival of richer structure.
